+# Design: pre-generated message ids and persistence status on `done`
+
+## Goal
+
+- Clients learn the ids of both messages of a chat turn from the start of the
+  stream: the ids are generated when the turn is opened, and the assistant
+  message id is sent on the first `conversation` event.
+- The final `done` event says whether the turn was saved (`persisted`), which
+  message it was saved as (`message_id`, `None` when saving failed) and when
+  (`created_at`, ISO timestamp of the stored assistant message).
+- `done` is the last and only completion event. A failed save no longer emits a
+  separate `error` event; the answer is still delivered.
+
+## Scope
+
+Included:
+
+- `ChatSession` / `ChatService.open_turn` (application/chat/service.py).
+- `build_turn_messages` (domain/conversation/turns.py) and every caller.
+- `ChatTurnResult` (also the response model of `POST /api/v1/chat`).
+- Removal of `EventType.ERROR`, `ProgressEvent.error`, `ErrorCode.PERSIST_FAILED`
+  and `PERSIST_FAILED_MESSAGE`.
+- CLI event rendering (`cli._render`).
+- README sentence describing the SSE stream.
+
+Unchanged:
+
+- The runner (`ChatTurnRunner`) and its own `done` payload (`run_id`,
+  `conversation_id`, `status`, `error_code`, `usage`); the session only adds keys.
+- Persistence contract (`ConversationRepository.append_turn`), message ordering
+  (assistant `created_at` = user `created_at` + 1 µs), audit records.
+- `ChatTurnResult.message_id` keeps its meaning (assistant id or `None`).
+- All other event types and their payloads; the `conversation` event keeps its
+  existing keys.
+- HTTP routes and their status codes.
+
+## Interfaces
+
+```python
+# domain/conversation/turns.py
+def build_turn_messages(
+    *,
+    conversation_id: str,
+    user_message_id: str,        # new, required
+    assistant_message_id: str,   # new, required
+    run: AgentRun,
+    answer_text: str,
+    citations: Sequence[Citation],
+    phases: Sequence[str],
+    now: datetime,
+) -> tuple[Message, Message]
+
+# application/chat/service.py
+class ChatTurnResult(BaseModel):
+    conversation_id: str
+    message_id: str | None
+    user_message_id: str          # new
+    assistant_message_id: str     # new
+    run_id: str
+    status: str
+    content: str
+    citations: list[Citation]
+    phases: list[str]
+    usage: dict[str, Any]
+    persisted: bool
+    created_at: datetime          # new: stored assistant message's created_at
+
+class ChatSession:
+    def __init__(self, execution, conversation, *, created, conversations, clock,
+                 user_message_id: str, assistant_message_id: str) -> None
+    @property
+    def user_message_id(self) -> str        # read-only
+    @property
+    def assistant_message_id(self) -> str   # read-only
+
+# ChatService.open_turn: unchanged signature; generates both ids with new_id().
+
+# application/progress.py
+class EventType(StrEnum): ...  # ERROR removed
+class ProgressEvent: ...        # error() classmethod removed
+
+# domain/agent/run.py
+class ErrorCode(StrEnum): ...   # PERSIST_FAILED removed
+```
+
+SSE payloads emitted by `ChatSession.events()`:
+
+- `conversation`: `{conversation_id, title, created, message_id}` where
+  `message_id` = assistant message id.
+- `done`: runner payload plus `{conversation_id, message_id, persisted, created_at}`;
+  `message_id` = assistant id if persisted else `None`; `created_at` =
+  `assistant_message.created_at.isoformat()`.
+
+## Approach
+
+- Id generation belongs to the application layer (`ChatService.open_turn`),
+  which already owns the session lifecycle; it calls `new_id()` twice (uuid4,
+  so distinct) and passes the ids into `ChatSession`, which stores them in
+  private attributes exposed as properties. The domain builder becomes pure with
+  respect to ids: it receives them as keyword arguments.
+- `ChatSession._persist` passes its ids to `build_turn_messages` and fills the
+  new `ChatTurnResult` fields. `created_at` is the assistant message's
+  `created_at` (it is set regardless of the save outcome, since the message was
+  built).
+- `events()` adds `message_id` to the first event and `persisted` / `created_at`
+  to `done`, and stops emitting the error event. Removing `EventType.ERROR`
+  and `ProgressEvent.error` is safe: the only producer was this code path; the
+  CLI branch that rendered `error` events is replaced.
+- CLI: `_render` prints `!! Không lưu được lượt hội thoại này.` to stderr when a
+  `done` event has `persisted` set to `False` (checked with `is False`, so the
+  runner's own `done`, which has no `persisted` key, prints nothing). Nothing
+  else on `done` goes to stderr.
+
+## Testing
+
+- Domain: `build_turn_messages` uses the given ids (tests/domain/test_conversation.py).
+  Existing callers in tests are updated to pass ids.
+- Application (tests/application/test_chat_service.py):
+  - session exposes two distinct ids; first event carries the assistant id;
+    stored messages use the ids; `done` has `persisted=True`, matching
+    `message_id` and `created_at`; result fields match.
+  - persist failure: `done` is the last event, `message_id is None`,
+    `persisted is False`, no `error` event, answer still delivered.
+  - `EventType` has no `ERROR` member; `ErrorCode` has no `PERSIST_FAILED`.
+- API (tests/api/test_chat_api.py): stream `done` carries `persisted` and
+  `created_at`; non-stream body has the new fields.
+- CLI (tests/test_cli.py): `_render` of a `done` event with `persisted=False`
+  writes the message to stderr; with `persisted=True` or no key writes nothing.
+- Full suite: `cd backend && uv run pytest`.
